another scientifically inaccurate and  potentially harmful statement made by  another celebrity about health it's no  surprise but we're talking about it on  this week's Wednesday checkup I found  out last week that Lady gaga  sat down to have an open and honest  conversation with Oprah about her mental  health this conversation talked about  her own journey breaking the mental  health stigma that exists and addressing  disparities now all those things are  incredibly important and I'm actually  really passionate about addressing those  issues in fact many of my patients who  are uninsured or only have catastrophic  insurance they face a really tough task  in finding a mental health counselor  therapist psychiatrist and it's my job  as a family medicine doctor it's not  only fill that void but also help them  navigate this really tricky system that  we have here in the United States when  it comes to mental health stigma this  absolutely needs to be broken but in  order to break the mental health stigma  we need honest and accurate information  when we talk about someone having a  mental health issue that's not to say  that they're weak it's not to say that  there's something wrong with them but  diagnosing ourselves is not the solution  reading a book and saying oh I must have  X Y &amp; Z and now I need to self treat  myself also is not ideal the most ideal  way to go about getting proper mental  health treatment is a to see a licensed  therapist psychiatrist or primary care  physician and I say primary care  physician because there is a specific  segment within Lady Gaga's speech that  is harmfully inaccurate by the way if  your primary care doctor is prescribing  you an antidepressant this should not be  happening primary care doctors should be  introducing you to a psychiatrist and  expert in brain medication now as a  primary care doctor a board-certified  family medicine physician my problem  with this statement is not personal  offense lady gaga  did not offend me by saying this  statement but what she is saying is  scientifically inaccurate and it's wrong  factually let's start off on a practical  note let's talk about the numbers the  National Institutes of Health estimates  that one in five adults will have a  mental health issue  when I say issue a classifiable mental  health disorder that equates to about 40  to 66 million individuals with a  condition now there's only about 20  something thousand practicing  psychiatrists in the u.s. how are all  those people going to get care and on  top of that there's certain geographic  limitations where there are no  practicing psychiatrists in a given area  then there's people who have lower  socioeconomic standing who are uninsured  and have higher rates of mental illness  what are they supposed to do Lady gaga  second we don't want to put even more  stress on our overburdened mental  healthcare system  think about this psychiatrists are  specialists in what they do and they  should be seeing more complex cases if  every single time any patient that  needed antidepressant needed to be  referred to them think about the  incredible way times that that would  create for patients who are truly  complex and can't be treated by family  medicine doctors this will completely  break already nearly broken system in  fact psychiatrists and other specialists  that I've worked with in the past have  sometimes laid the complaint at my feet  that some family medicine providers are  too quick to refer instead of trying  first-line treatments like  antidepressants and I completely agree  with that which goes completely against  what Lady Gaga was trying to say in this  speech three board certified family  medicine doctors receive extensive  education and training in mental health  conditions and brain medications with  the ability to refer or triage as needed  in reality it's the actual job of the  family medicine doctor to be able to  administer first line and even second  line treatments before sending the  patient to a specialist if you try the  first line treatment it fails maybe you  try the second line treatment it fails  then you need a specialist if you need  to perform a procedure that you're not  trained in or you don't have the  equipment to perform you need to send to  a specialist if you've come across a  complex case with a patient having more  than one illness or taking more than one  medication that could be high enough  complexity to warrant a referral perhaps  you're not comfortable with the specific  illness that the patient has and you  don't have enough experience with it  that warrants our referral but these are  the minority of cases the huge majority  of patients  anxiety and depression who warned  antidepressants can and should be  treated by primary care doctors also as  a side note not every patient with  anxiety and oppression needs  antidepressants and in fact that's the  beauty of family medicine we understand  that cognitive behavioral therapy DBT  all these options work very well for our  patients and because we have existing  relationships with these patients  because we're their primary care  providers we treat them for years we  know what works we know what medications  they do well with they have direct  access to us and it only makes sense  that we continue this line of treatment  until it passes that complexity line  where we do need to get a specialist  like a psychiatrist involved for I think  there's a faulty mentality at the heart  of this statement where more money spent  always yields better care or outcomes  and that's just not the case in reality  I have many celebrity or wealthy  patients that come in to see me that  request every single test or every  single treatment and I have to explain  to them that tests come with possible  risks treatments come with possible  risks even though some of them may seem  completely benign like a blood test and  we need to have those conversations now  you may be wondering to yourself how is  seeing a specialist putting me at risk  well it's not directly putting you at  risk because specialists are very  confident they know what they're doing  but from my experience what I've seen is  if a patient who does not have a primary  care doctor gets sent to a specialist  too early they sometimes get treatments  or even tests for things that they don't  need yet this is not because the  specialist don't know what they're doing  or because they're trying to push things  on to the patient it's because they're  used to seeing more complex cases so  they have a lower threshold of when to  order certain tests or certain  treatments and they don't have existing  relationships with these patients like  we do as primary care providers for  example I have some patients that when  they come in and they say their pain  levels of four or five I get really  worried because I already know they have  a very high pain threshold but if they  go see a cardiologist they say oh my  pain is a for maybe it's not taken as  seriously if you jump on to any review  site you'll see patients complaining  that they didn't get  or treatments from their doctor and they  think that's a bad thing but I recently  came across a tweet from a dr. Christina  Johns that I think works very well in  this case it reads just because no tests  were performed or medicines prescribed  it doesn't mean that you didn't get care  I completely agree with the statement  because it takes careful evaluation to  know what a patient has to weigh the  risks and benefits of each procedure and  each treatment and figure out what's  right for the individual sitting in  front of you that's why generalizations  don't work well in medicine and that's  why Lady gaga statement here does more  harm than good at the end of the day it  puts patients at risk who would  otherwise go get care from a family  medicine doctor it hurts existing  doctor-patient relationships that are  potentially going very well I guess the  final thing that really makes a  statement inaccurate is that pretty much  every medical organization agrees that  family medicine doctors are more than  capable and are trained to prescribe  antidepressants to know when to refer  patients if the complexity arises and to  make a universal statement like that  that disagrees with all of medicine just  doesn't make a lot of sense just four  days ago the active president of the AFP  dr. Leroy made a great statement family  medicine physicians are on the front  lines of mental health care in America  and I completely agree with that  statement do you know why I would  actually go as far as to say 80% of my  visits there is a mental health care  component so when a patient comes in for  back pain acid reflux diabetes chronic  care management those usually have a  mental health component in them whether  we see it or not whether we diagnose it  or not and if we're gonna go ahead and  make a universal statement and say  family medicine doctors shouldn't  prescribe antidepressants or they're  incapable to take care of mental health  it just flat-out wrong I'm dropping some  great resources down below including a  response from the American Academy of  Family Physicians and a fantastic open  letter by dr. Megan Babb on the subject  I encourage you to read it a lot of the  same points I brought up in this video  were addressed in her letter as well and  we really should send a message to Lady  gaga and really all influential  celebrities out there your message is  going to be heard by millions of people  they're gonna trust you because you're a  familiar  face they have respect for you so if  you're going to be making Universal  recommendations about health care please  PLEASE partner with a medical  organization partner with a medical  professional before more harm than good  is done with those statements if you're  interested to watch some of my other  videos about mental health which I'm  very passionate about as an osteopathic  and holistic physician click here as  always stay happy and healthy  [Music]
 

